# =============================================================================

# =============================================================================

# PHASE SCOPE RESTRICTION

# =============================================================================

This phase audits only data and information infrastructure implemented inside
the current Core scope.

Primary scope:

src/agent/core/

src/agent/components/core/

The audit shall not assume the existence of:

semantic memory

episodic memory

working memory

autobiographical memory

cognitive knowledge graphs

learning datasets

model-training pipelines

cognitive-state governance

agent capability networks

distributed cognition

perception pipelines

reasoning traces

These systems shall be classified as:

OUT OF CURRENT SCOPE

PLANNED

NOT YET IMPLEMENTED

NOT AUDITABLE

where applicable.

Current Core data-governance concerns may include:

configuration

runtime state

component state

service state

task infrastructure

lifecycle records

registries

events

messages

queues

logs

telemetry

diagnostics

checkpoints

runtime artifacts

resource metadata

structural metadata

package metadata

integrity reports

# =============================================================================

# 59. PROVENANCE AUTHORITY

# =============================================================================

Locate the canonical authority responsible for information provenance.

Search for:

ProvenanceManager

ProvenanceRegistry

ProvenanceService

LineageManager

TraceabilityManager

ArtifactProvenance

SourceMetadata

Determine:

owner

scope

construction

public API

storage

lifecycle

validation

If no dedicated provenance authority exists, determine whether provenance is
owned by individual data-producing subsystems.

Report fragmented ownership.

# =============================================================================

# 60. PROVENANCE MODEL

# =============================================================================

Recover the canonical provenance model.

Possible fields:

record identifier

source identifier

source type

producer

producer version

creation timestamp

collection timestamp

transformation timestamp

runtime identifier

task identifier

component identifier

service identifier

configuration revision

parent records

transformation history

integrity hash

classification

retention policy

Determine:

required fields

optional fields

field ownership

field types

serialization

validation

# =============================================================================

# 61. PROVENANCE RECORD IDENTITY

# =============================================================================

Determine how provenance records are identified.

Possible mechanisms:

UUID

content hash

monotonic sequence

composite identifier

source-relative identifier

artifact-relative identifier

Determine:

uniqueness scope

stability

collision handling

persistence

reconstruction

# =============================================================================

# 62. SOURCE IDENTITY

# =============================================================================

Determine how information sources are represented.

Possible sources:

operator input

configuration file

environment variable

runtime component

service

provider

tool

filesystem

network endpoint

generated process

restored checkpoint

Determine whether source identity distinguishes:

human-provided information

machine-generated information

derived information

restored information

externally retrieved information

# =============================================================================

# 63. PRODUCER IDENTITY

# =============================================================================

Determine whether every generated record can identify its producer.

Possible producers:

module

package

component

service

kernel

scheduler

resource manager

integrity checker

configuration loader

diagnostic subsystem

Determine whether producer identity includes:

canonical path

component ID

service ID

implementation version

runtime instance

# =============================================================================

# 64. CREATION PROVENANCE

# =============================================================================

Determine whether information creation records:

creation authority

creation time

input sources

active configuration

runtime version

schema version

initial classification

initial retention policy

# =============================================================================

# 65. TRANSFORMATION PROVENANCE

# =============================================================================

Recover provenance for transformations such as:

normalization

validation

serialization

deserialization

aggregation

filtering

redaction

compression

migration

conversion

merging

splitting

Determine whether transformed outputs retain links to all contributing inputs.

# =============================================================================

# 66. DERIVATION CHAINS

# =============================================================================

Determine whether derived information preserves a complete derivation chain.

Possible chain:

source

collection

validation

normalization

transformation

storage

export

deletion

Determine whether the chain is:

complete

partial

best effort

not implemented

# =============================================================================

# 67. PROVENANCE GRANULARITY

# =============================================================================

Determine provenance granularity.

Possible levels:

package

module

service

component

task

event

record

field

artifact

Determine the intended granularity for each Core information domain.

# =============================================================================

# 68. PROVENANCE IMMUTABILITY

# =============================================================================

Determine whether provenance records are:

immutable

append-only

mutable with revision history

fully mutable

derived on demand

Determine who may alter provenance.

A producer shall not silently rewrite historical source information.

# =============================================================================

# 69. PROVENANCE VERSIONING

# =============================================================================

Determine whether provenance records contain:

schema version

producer version

transformation version

source version

runtime version

configuration version

Determine migration behavior after schema changes.

# =============================================================================

# 70. PROVENANCE PERSISTENCE

# =============================================================================

Determine where provenance is stored.

Possible locations:

inside the record

sidecar metadata

central registry

database table

artifact manifest

log stream

checkpoint manifest

Determine consistency between information and its provenance.

# =============================================================================

# 71. PROVENANCE LOSS

# =============================================================================

Identify operations that may discard provenance.

Examples:

serialization to simplified formats

copying values without metadata

aggregating records

exporting artifacts

checkpoint restoration

queue transport

event conversion

legacy adapter conversion

Report every lossy boundary.

# =============================================================================

# 72. LINEAGE AUTHORITY

# =============================================================================

Locate the canonical authority for data lineage.

Determine whether lineage is:

explicitly stored

derived from provenance

reconstructed from logs

represented by parent identifiers

not implemented

# =============================================================================

# 73. LINEAGE GRAPH

# =============================================================================

Recover lineage relationships among:

sources

records

events

messages

tasks

components

services

checkpoints

artifacts

reports

Generate a Mermaid lineage graph.

# =============================================================================

# 74. UPSTREAM LINEAGE

# =============================================================================

Determine whether a record can identify:

immediate source

original source

all contributing sources

configuration source

runtime producer

# =============================================================================

# 75. DOWNSTREAM LINEAGE

# =============================================================================

Determine whether the architecture can identify consumers and descendants of a
record.

Possible consumers:

services

components

tasks

registries

logs

diagnostics

exports

checkpoints

Determine whether downstream lineage is explicit or inferred.

# =============================================================================

# 76. CROSS-SERVICE LINEAGE

# =============================================================================

Determine whether lineage survives movement across service boundaries.

Inspect:

method calls

events

queues

messages

serialization

shared state

registries

Determine where correlation identifiers are preserved or lost.

# =============================================================================

# 77. CROSS-PROCESS LINEAGE

# =============================================================================

Where Core supports multiple processes or daemons, determine whether lineage
survives:

IPC

sockets

message brokers

shared files

shared databases

subprocess execution

Do not fail the audit where such architecture is not yet implemented.

Classify unsupported paths as:

NOT APPLICABLE

NOT YET IMPLEMENTED

# =============================================================================

