# =============================================================================

# 191. DATA ACCEPTANCE GATES

# =============================================================================

Define gates before information enters canonical Core state.

DATA-GATE-01

Schema is recognized.

DATA-GATE-02

Required fields are present.

DATA-GATE-03

Types are valid.

DATA-GATE-04

Identifiers are valid.

DATA-GATE-05

References resolve.

DATA-GATE-06

Source is identified.

DATA-GATE-07

Provenance is sufficient.

DATA-GATE-08

Classification is assigned where required.

DATA-GATE-09

Retention policy is assigned where required.

DATA-GATE-10

Integrity validation passes.

# =============================================================================

# 192. PRIVACY ACCEPTANCE GATES

# =============================================================================

Define:

PRIVACY-GATE-01

Sensitive fields are identified where supported.

PRIVACY-GATE-02

Secrets are not emitted to logs.

PRIVACY-GATE-03

Diagnostics apply required redaction.

PRIVACY-GATE-04

Exports enforce scope and redaction.

PRIVACY-GATE-05

Optional telemetry is explicitly controlled.

PRIVACY-GATE-06

Retention is bounded.

PRIVACY-GATE-07

Deletion paths exist where required.

PRIVACY-GATE-08

Access boundaries are explicit.

# =============================================================================

# 193. PROVENANCE ACCEPTANCE GATES

# =============================================================================

Define:

PROVENANCE-GATE-01

Every governed record identifies its producer.

PROVENANCE-GATE-02

Every external input identifies its source category.

PROVENANCE-GATE-03

Transformations preserve parent references where required.

PROVENANCE-GATE-04

Artifact manifests preserve source information.

PROVENANCE-GATE-05

Checkpoint provenance is complete.

PROVENANCE-GATE-06

Configuration provenance identifies override sources.

PROVENANCE-GATE-07

Provenance survives serialization.

PROVENANCE-GATE-08

Provenance loss is explicit and justified.

# =============================================================================

# 194. LINEAGE ACCEPTANCE GATES

# =============================================================================

Define:

LINEAGE-GATE-01

Task ancestry is recoverable.

LINEAGE-GATE-02

Event causation is recoverable where events are implemented.

LINEAGE-GATE-03

Message correlation survives transport where messaging is implemented.

LINEAGE-GATE-04

Artifacts link to producing inputs.

LINEAGE-GATE-05

Checkpoints link to source runtime state.

LINEAGE-GATE-06

Structural metadata links to its governing schema or generator where applicable.

# =============================================================================

# 195. RETENTION ACCEPTANCE GATES

# =============================================================================

Define:

RETENTION-GATE-01

Governed information has an explicit retention category.

RETENTION-GATE-02

Expiration can be determined.

RETENTION-GATE-03

Expiration is enforced.

RETENTION-GATE-04

Overrides are auditable.

RETENTION-GATE-05

Archives preserve classification and integrity.

RETENTION-GATE-06

Expired temporary information is removed.

# =============================================================================

# 196. DELETION ACCEPTANCE GATES

# =============================================================================

Define:

DELETE-GATE-01

Deletion authority is explicit.

DELETE-GATE-02

Deletion scope is explicit.

DELETE-GATE-03

Primary records are removed or tombstoned according to policy.

DELETE-GATE-04

Indexes and caches are reconciled.

DELETE-GATE-05

Replicas are handled where applicable.

DELETE-GATE-06

Deletion outcome is verified.

DELETE-GATE-07

Partial failures are reported.

DELETE-GATE-08

Deletion audit metadata does not retain prohibited content.

# =============================================================================

# 197. STATIC VERIFICATION

# =============================================================================

Perform static analysis limited to the current Core scope.

Search for:

records without stable identifiers

records without source information

untyped data contracts

unchecked dictionaries

duplicate schema definitions

implicit schema versions

unsafe deserialization

pickle use

unredacted logging

environment-variable dumping

credential leakage

absolute user-specific paths

unbounded retention

temporary files without cleanup

orphan artifacts

checkpoint files without manifests

exports without provenance

deletion without verification

shared mutable state without ownership

legacy structural metadata schemas

incomplete **tree**.py contracts

Do not modify production implementation.

# =============================================================================

# 198. RUNTIME VERIFICATION

# =============================================================================

Where safe and supported, verify:

contract validation

serialization round trips

invalid-field rejection

unknown-schema rejection

configuration-source tracking

checkpoint-manifest generation

retention expiration

temporary-file cleanup

redaction behavior

deletion verification

Do not activate unavailable higher-level subsystems.

Do not require external services unless Core tests explicitly provide them.

# =============================================================================

# 199. DATA GOVERNANCE FINDING CLASSIFICATION

# =============================================================================

Classify findings as:

CRITICAL

HIGH

MEDIUM

LOW

INFORMATIONAL

Examples:

CRITICAL

credentials serialized into persistent diagnostics

unsafe deserialization allowing arbitrary code execution

deletion reports success while sensitive records remain accessible

untrusted information enters canonical runtime state without validation in a
security-critical path

HIGH

checkpoints lack schema and provenance metadata

configuration exports expose secrets

Core data contracts conflict across services

retention policies are absent for persistent diagnostics

incomplete structural contracts prevent package-integrity enforcement

MEDIUM

event causation IDs are absent

schema versions are implicit

temporary files rely only on normal shutdown cleanup

classification propagation is inconsistent

provenance is lost during aggregation

LOW

inconsistent timestamp formatting

duplicate descriptive metadata

unused optional schema fields

stale documentation

INFORMATIONAL

ephemeral records intentionally lack persistence

cross-process lineage is not applicable

privacy export support is intentionally deferred

# =============================================================================

# 200. PART II ACCEPTANCE INVARIANTS

# =============================================================================

Evaluate:

DATA-009

Every canonical Core data contract has one owner.

DATA-010

Every persistent Core record has a stable schema.

DATA-011

Every persistent Core record identifies its source or producer.

DATA-012

Derived Core information preserves required provenance.

DATA-013

Task, event and artifact lineage is recoverable where implemented.

DATA-014

Configuration values preserve source precedence.

DATA-015

Serialization is deterministic where integrity hashing depends upon it.

DATA-016

Deserialization does not execute untrusted code.

DATA-017

Required references are validated.

DATA-018

Identifiers are unique within their declared scope.

DATA-019

Classification is explicit where sensitive data may exist.

DATA-020

Secrets are redacted before logging or diagnostics.

DATA-021

Optional telemetry is explicitly controlled.

DATA-022

Persistent information has an explicit retention policy.

DATA-023

Expired temporary information is cleaned up.

DATA-024

Deletion authority and scope are explicit.

DATA-025

Deletion success is verifiable.

DATA-026

Shared mutable information has one owner.

DATA-027

Exports preserve schema, provenance and classification metadata.

DATA-028

Checkpoints preserve schema, integrity and provenance metadata.

DATA-029

Structural metadata uses one supported contract schema.

DATA-030

Unsupported future data domains do not fail current Core certification.

